  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x={cos}^{2}\u2061x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061x}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}(1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
